---
import ToolPage from '../../layouts/ToolPage.astro';
import RackStackGame from '../../components/games/RackStackGame.tsx';
import { withBase } from '../../lib/url';
---

<ToolPage figure="cabinet" title="Rack & Stack" lede="Pick the accelerator, how many, and the precision to serve a model at the speed users need, for the least money.">
  <RackStackGame client:load />
  <Fragment slot="notes">
    <p class="gm-note"><strong>Specs checked October 2026.</strong> Figures are rounded vendor numbers; ones marked with a tilde were not re-verified. Newer parts (for example Blackwell Ultra, Trainium and TPU v6e) are covered in the lessons but not yet in this table. Always check the current datasheet before you buy.</p>
    <h2>How to play</h2>
    <p>Choose a GPU, a count and two precisions, watch the estimates move, then press <strong>Check my choice</strong>. You pass only if the model fits, every user gets enough speed, quality holds and the example cost is within budget. Passing scores 70 and up, and the cheapest valid choice scores 100.</p>
    <h2>What it teaches</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Memory decides <strong>whether</strong> a model runs. Bandwidth decides <strong>how fast</strong>. A cheap card can hold a model and still be too slow.</li>
      <li>With many users or long chats, the KV cache can be bigger than the weights.</li>
      <li>More cards add memory and bandwidth but cost communication. Cards without a fast link split badly.</li>
      <li>The cheapest answer is often not the newest or biggest GPU.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Prices are made-up examples. Background: <a href={withBase('/learn/infra/reading-a-gpu-spec-sheet/')}>Reading a GPU spec sheet</a> and the <a href={withBase('/tools/gpu/')}>GPU explorer</a>.</p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolPage>
